(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-study-design-research-methods-quiz"]={"title":"Study Design and Research Methods Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why must the results of a randomised trial be applicable to a larger population?","choices":["Journals will not publish a trial without it","It allows the trial to be conducted more cheaply","A finding confined to the sample cannot guide practice","It removes the need for a control group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is external validity by another name, which is why adequate sample size and a clear protocol are listed alongside it as requirements. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where is field research commonly used?","choices":["In epidemiology during investigations","Only in laboratory settings","Only in qualitative studies","Only in secondary analysis of existing data"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with the testing of new screening tests and vaccine trials given as examples. It may be experimental or observational depending on the question. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does a cohort study test?","choices":["Hypotheses","Only whether an association exists at one moment","The effect of an intervention the investigator assigned","The pooled effect across several published studies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cross-sectional design generates hypotheses, the case-control design generates or tests them, and the cohort design is described as testing them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which are examples of qualitative research?","choices":["Randomised controlled trials and cohort studies","Case study, biography, ethnography and phenomenology","Case-control studies and meta-analyses","Cross-sectional surveys and longitudinal studies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and none of them produces a number as its output. Randomised trials, cohort and case-control studies are the quantitative examples. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does pooling data in a meta-analysis achieve?","choices":["It removes bias present in the component studies","It produces what amounts to a single large study","It converts observational studies into experiments","It establishes the temporal sequence of exposure and outcome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a greater number of participants usually means greater power, though the qualifier matters: pooling weak studies gives a larger weak study. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is a longitudinal ecologic survey?","choices":["A single survey of a population at one moment","A trial in which exposure is assigned and followed","Ongoing surveillance or repeated surveys measuring trends","A study comparing cases with controls for past exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it may reveal an association, such as whether lung cancer incidence rises as smoking increases among women over time. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which characteristic could define a cohort?","choices":["Occupation, religious affiliation or date of birth","Only exposure to a suspected risk factor","Only the presence of the outcome under study","Only random assignment by the investigator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside age, exposure to a risk factor, having a specific disease, or taking part in a preventive or therapeutic intervention. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The 1996 Atlanta Olympic Games were followed by fewer emergency visits for childhood asthma. What kind of study is that?","choices":["A cross-sectional ecologic study","A prospective cohort study","A randomised controlled trial","A case-control study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: streets were closed, car emissions fell, and the association was drawn between population-level air pollution and population-level asthma visits. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does qualitative research aim to develop, and quantitative research?","choices":["Predictions and descriptions respectively","Both develop predictions","Both develop descriptions","Descriptions and predictions respectively"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the difference in aim follows from the reasoning: qualitative work is inductive, moving from observation to account, while quantitative work is deductive. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How should the comparison group in a cohort study be chosen?","choices":["At random from the general population","As similar as possible except for the exposure","To match the exposed group on outcome","To be as different as possible from the exposed group"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because any other difference between the groups becomes an alternative explanation for whatever difference in outcome appears. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the ecologic fallacy?","choices":["Applying individual data to a whole population","Assuming an association proves causation","Measuring exposure and outcome at the same time","Applying aggregate data to an individual"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the standing hazard of ecologic data: a relationship that holds between populations need not hold within any one person in them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How long must a cohort be followed?","choices":["Until the sample size reaches a predetermined figure","Exactly as long as the exposure lasted","Long enough for the expected outcome to occur","Until the first outcome event occurs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the requirement pairs with completeness: a cohort followed too briefly shows nothing, and one followed incompletely shows something distorted. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How is qualitative research characterised?","choices":["Positivistic and deductive, developing predictions","Experimental, with the investigator assigning the intervention","Naturalistic and inductive, developing descriptions","Secondary, analysing data others have collected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and its data are observation, field notes and audio recordings, drawn from both researchers and participants rather than from measurement. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What chain runs from study design to clinical conclusions?","choices":["Strong analysis gives strong data, which gives valid design","Large samples give strong design, which gives valid results","Valid results give strong data, which gives strong design","Strong design gives strong data, which gives valid results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the first link is the one that cannot be repaired later: data gathered under a weak design cannot be rescued by careful analysis afterwards. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What can a case-control study be used for?","choices":["Generating hypotheses only","Establishing causation definitively","Generating or testing hypotheses","Measuring the effect of an assigned intervention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which sets it apart from the cross-sectional design: that one generates hypotheses only, while a case-control study can also test them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What defines an experimental design?","choices":["The exposure is observed rather than assigned","Participants are followed forward over time","Groups are defined by their outcome","The investigator assigns the intervention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the randomised controlled trial is the example. Where the exposure is merely observed rather than assigned, the design is observational. The investigator manipulates it as well. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which are given as examples of field research?","choices":["Systematic reviews of published trials","Retrospective analysis of hospital discharge records","Testing new screening tests and vaccine trials","Interviews conducted for a phenomenological study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and both take place in the population rather than the laboratory, which is what makes them field work during epidemiologic investigation. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why can a cross-sectional study not establish which came first?","choices":["The sample is too small to detect a sequence","Risk factor and disease are measured at the same time","The data are drawn from population level aggregates","Participants are lost to follow-up"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and determining temporal sequence is named as something a good design should permit, which is why this one generates hypotheses rather than testing them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which design can establish temporal sequence most directly?","choices":["A cross-sectional study","A case series","A cohort study, starting from exposure","An ecologic study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the exposure is recorded before the outcome occurs, which a cross-sectional design measuring both at once cannot do. It then waits for the outcome to appear. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is a cohort?","choices":["A group defined by whether it has the disease","A group randomly assigned to an intervention","A population measured at a single point in time","A group with a common characteristic, followed over time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the shared characteristic can be almost anything: age, date of birth, exposure to a risk factor, occupation, religious affiliation, a disease, or taking part in an intervention. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why does understanding study design help clinical decision making?","choices":["It allows the clinician to calculate the results independently","Knowing each design's strengths and limits","It identifies which journal will accept the study","It removes the need to consider patient preferences"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and better evaluation of evidence is what makes the resulting clinical decision more informed rather than merely better referenced. It is what improves the evaluation of evidence. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which designs are given as examples of analytic work?","choices":["Randomised trials, cohort and case-control studies","Case reports and case series","Systematic reviews and meta-analyses","Ethnography and phenomenology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and what unites them is comparison: each sets one group against another, which is what supports an inference about a population. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does a descriptive design do?","choices":["Compares characteristics across two or more groups","Describes the characteristics of a group of people","Assigns an intervention to participants","Follows a defined group forward over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its examples are the case report and the case series. Analytic designs instead compare two or more groups to draw an inference about a population. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How is field research classified?","choices":["As observational only","As experimental only","As qualitative only","As either experimental or observational"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, unlike longitudinal work, surveys and policy evaluation, which are all classed as observational. It depends on whether anything is assigned. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is a systematic review built to answer?","choices":["A specific, focused question","As many related questions as the literature allows","Whether an assigned intervention works","How common a condition is at one moment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it does so by systematically identifying and evaluating multiple studies rather than by surveying a field impressionistically. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the goal of epidemiologic and clinical research design?","choices":["An unbiased comparison between groups with and without the factor","Proving that the factor causes the outcome in every case","Enrolling as many participants as possible","Describing one group of patients in complete detail"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and everything a good design permits follows from that aim: quantifying the risk difference, determining temporal sequence and minimising bias. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does a retrospective cohort study work?","choices":["The investigator assembles groups now and waits for outcomes","The investigator identifies a past cohort and follows it to the present","The investigator starts from the outcome and looks backward","The investigator measures exposure and outcome at the same moment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: exposures are determined at that historical point, and the group is followed forward from there to now to determine outcome. It is also called a historical cohort study. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"On what basis are subjects grouped in a cohort study?","choices":["Outcome rather than exposure","Random allocation by the investigator","The order in which they enrolled","Exposure rather than outcome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the comparison group should be as similar as possible except for that exposure, which is what makes any difference in outcome attributable to it. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What features are required of a randomised controlled trial?","choices":["A clear hypothesis, a protocol, and random assignment","A cohort assembled by exposure and followed forward","Groups defined by whether the outcome has occurred","Measurement of exposure and outcome at one moment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, together with an adequate sample size, results applicable to a larger population, and an accounting for withdrawals and drop-outs. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is secondary research?","choices":["Data the researcher collects personally","Analysis of existing data","Research conducted after an initial pilot study","Research with a second control group"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with meta-analyses and systematic review articles as the examples. Primary research uses data the investigator collected personally. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"}]};
